TI  - [Tracheal Tumours].
PG  - 286-297
LID - 10.1055/a-2223-1175 [doi]
AB  - Malignant primary tracheal tumours are rare. The most common histological 
      subtypes are squamous cell carcinoma and adenoid cystic carcinoma. These two 
      entities have different prognoses and growth patterns. Tracheobronchoscopy and 
      thoracic sectional imaging are standard diagnostic tools for tumour staging and 
      local evaluation. Complete surgical resection of the affected tracheal segment is 
      the treatment of choice for limited disease without distant metastases. 
      Incomplete gross tumour resection with additional irradiation is an acceptable 
      therapeutic option for adenoid cystic carcinoma. Interventional endoscopy with 
      tumour debulking or tracheal stenting and/or definitive mediastinal radiotherapy 
      are treatment alternatives in either a locally advanced or palliative setting.
