TI  - Free Swimming and Exercise-Induced Pulmonary Hemorrhage in Endurance Horses: A 
      Preliminary Study.
PG  - 104182
LID - S0737-0806(22)00318-5 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jevs.2022.104182 [doi]
AB  - Swimming is used for rehabilitation of musculoskeletal injuries and for 
      conditioning to improve equine fitness. However, there are anecdotal reports that 
      suggest that tethered swimming can induce epistaxis, likely secondary to 
      exercise-induced pulmonary hemorrhage (EIPH). The objective of this 
      observational, prospective study was to investigate if EIPH occurs during 
      intensive free-swimming training sessions (5 × 70m) using 15 endurance horses. On 
      tracheo-bronchoscopic evaluations following swimming, low grade mucus scores were 
      observed, but no tracheal blood was observed. Bronchoalveolar lavage fluid 
      analysis revealed a low cellularity, and the median red blood cell count (RBCs) 
      was 271 cells/μL (interquartile range 150-363 cells/μL), which is much lower than 
      the threshold of RBCs >1,000 cells/μL for horses to be considered positive for 
      EIPH. Therefore, free swimming does not seem to predispose endurance horses to 
      EIPH following a typical free-swimming training session.
