TI  - Endobronchial Sialolipoma: An Uncommon Salivary Gland Tumor at an Uncommon Site.
PG  - 1593-1597
LID - 10.1177/10668969231160259 [doi]
AB  - BACKGROUND: Sialolipoma is a benign neoplasm composed of neoplastic adipose 
      tissue and normal salivary gland parenchyma. It is commonly seen in the parotid 
      gland. The occurrence of sialolipoma in the main bronchus is exceedingly rare. 
      CASE REPORT: A 52-year-old diabetic and hypertensive gentleman presented with 
      shortness of breath and cough for the last 3-4 months. Computed tomography 
      bronchial angiography showed a soft tissue lesion in the right intermediate 
      bronchus causing its complete obliteration and subsequent collapse of the right 
      lower lobe. A rigid tracheobronchoscope revealed a polypoidal growth originating 
      in the right intermediate bronchus. Histopathology revealed a sialolipoma. The 
      patient was doing well on follow-up and no recurrence was noted to date. 
      CONCLUSION: The bronchus as the primary site for sialolipoma is unusual and has 
      to be kept in the differential while dealing with the slow-growing endobronchial 
      tumor.
