TI  - Rigid tracheobronchoscopy in the management of airway foreign bodies: 10 years 
      experience in Kosovo.
PG  - 2055-9
AB  - OBJECTIVES: To review the incidence of tracheobronchial foreign body aspiration 
      in the population of Kosovo and to evaluate the clinical features and management 
      of tracheobronchial foreign body aspiration during a 10-year period. SETTING: 
      Tertiary medical institution, ENT/Head and Neck Surgery Clinic, University 
      Clinical Center, Prishtina, Kosovo. METHODS: We retrospectively reviewed the 
      medical records of patients with tracheobronchial foreign body aspiration who 
      underwent rigid tracheobronchoscopy between January 1994 and December 2003. 
      RESULTS: During a 10-year period, 244 rigid tracheobronchoscopy procedures were 
      performed when foreign body aspiration was suspected. In 182 patients (75%), a 
      foreign body was found and extirpated. Of these, 107 (59%) were male and 75 (41%) 
      were female; 124 patients (68%) were aged 0-3 years and 36 (20%) were aged 4-7 
      years. The most commonly extirpated foreign bodies were peanuts (30% of cases) 
      followed by beans (22.5%) and corn kernels (9%). Of 176 plain chest radiographs, 
      65% showed no signs of foreign bodies. Two patients (1.1%) died as result of 
      complications related to foreign body aspiration. CONCLUSION: Rigid bronchoscopy 
      represents a safe and effective tool in the management of foreign body 
      aspiration. However, the high percentage of false-negative results on chest 
      radiography in our series warrants further investigation.
